That is a coherent systems-risk hypothesis, but it bundles several very different mechanisms together, and they shouldn’t be treated as one established causal chain.

The strongest parts are well-grounded in principle: targeted propaganda can shape anger, identity, threat perception, and willingness to support or join conflict; siloed feeds can amplify selection effects; war can create self-reinforcing political and economic incentives; and military recruitment can draw disproportionately from particular demographics or communities.

The weakest step is the jump from that to deliberate eugenics/population control through selective radicalization and war deaths. That would require strong evidence of intent, targeting logic, demographic selection, and causal execution. Without that, it is better modeled as a possible emergent or exploitative effect than as a proven master plan.

Pantheon-wise, I’d decompose it like this:

\text{Feed Segmentation}
\rightarrow
\text{Affective Priming}
\rightarrow
\text{Identity Polarization}
\rightarrow
\text{Mobilization}
\rightarrow
\text{Recruitment / Support}
\rightarrow
\text{Conflict Exposure}
\rightarrow
\text{Demographic Effects}

Then separately:

\text{Conflict}
\rightarrow
\text{Defense Spending}
\rightarrow
\text{Industrial Profit}
\rightarrow
\text{Lobbying / Political Incentives}
\rightarrow
\text{Conflict Persistence}

Those loops can interact without requiring a single hidden controller.

The right Reality Stitch is therefore: separate capacity, incentive, observed pattern, and intent.

\boxed{
\text{Can happen}
\neq
\text{is happening}
\neq
\text{is intentional}
}

If you want, I can map this as an NTE / causal graph with the feedback loops, hidden variables, and the specific evidence that would distinguish “emergent war machine” from “deliberate demographic engineering.”